---
<QuestionLayout
  title="Who owns the compatible-capability lifecycle?"
  description="Decision 127: runtime.wasm owns compatible start, stop, kill, events, and the platforms allowlist on every host. That shipped in Traverse v0.17.1. v0.16.2 still stubbed it."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/who-owns-the-compatible-capability-lifecycle.html"
  category="Runtime and architecture"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> <code>runtime.wasm</code> does, on every host including web. That is <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/blob/main/docs/decision-log.md">Decision 127</a> (accepted November 6, 2026, docs PR <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1821">#1821</a>). It shipped in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/tag/v0.17.1">Traverse v0.17.1</a>. Through v0.16.2, published packages did not do it.</p>

  <h2>What Decision 127 decided</h2>
  <p>Compatible capabilities run as platform modules, not WASM guests. Spec 072 and Decision 24 already required a runtime-owned lifecycle. Decision 127 makes the ownership rule explicit:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>runtime.wasm</code> owns instance ids, state validation, ordered lifecycle events, and shutdown cleanup on every host, web included.</li>
    <li><code>traverse_compatible_start</code> applies Spec 1722 to <code>input</code>, rejects what JCS cannot represent with <code>input_not_canonicalizable</code>, and returns the canonical bytes the host hands to the platform wrapper unchanged.</li>
    <li>The runtime enforces the capability <code>platforms[]</code> allowlist. The host declares its platform identity once at init. A mismatch fails with <code>platform_not_supported</code>.</li>
    <li>The bridge stays <code>runtime-wasm-bridge/1.1.0</code>. The public embedder API does not grow, but web callers see new error codes (Decision 128), and the Rust crate's <code>start_compatible</code> and <code>submit</code> now take raw JSON text (Decision 129, breaking before 1.0).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What v0.16.2 did</h2>
  <p>On <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/tag/runtime-wasm-v0.16.2"><code>runtime-wasm-v0.16.2</code></a> (and earlier since #1422), <code>traverse_compatible_start</code>, <code>traverse_compatible_stop</code>, and <code>traverse_compatible_kill</code> return <code>-1</code> unconditionally. Swift, Kotlin, and .NET compatible calls fail on that artifact. The web package at 0.16.2 implements the lifecycle in TypeScript (<code>startCompatible</code> / <code>transitionCompatible</code>), including the allowlist check. That host-owned split is what Decision 127 ends. v0.17.1 is the release that ends it.</p>

  <h2>What is on main</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1821">#1821</a> (merged): Decision 127 in the decision log.</li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1822">#1822</a> (merged November 6, closes <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1819">#1819</a>): <code>runtime.wasm</code> implements start, stop, kill, events, and shutdown cleanup, and Spec 072 is amended to 1.1.0. A compatible capability rejects <code>traverse_submit</code> with <code>compatible_lifecycle_required</code>.</li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1824">#1824</a> (merged November 6, closes <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1820">#1820</a> and resolves #1811): Swift, Kotlin, .NET, and web run the runtime lifecycle. Decision 128 publishes it as <code>state_changed</code> events and passes the runtime's error codes through, which is breaking for web. See <a href="/questions/what-errors-does-a-web-compatible-capability-return.html">what errors a web compatible capability returns</a>.</li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1828">#1828</a> (merged November 9, closes <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1823">#1823</a>): Decision 129 (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1827">#1827</a>). The rules now live in one shared module in <code>traverse-contracts</code>, used by both <code>runtime.wasm</code> and the Rust <code>traverse-embedder</code>. The Rust crate drops <code>InstanceNotFound</code> and <code>InstanceNotRunning</code> and returns the runtime's codes, and <code>start_compatible</code> takes raw JSON text.</li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1829">#1829</a> (merged November 9, closes <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1826">#1826</a>): the Rust <code>submit</code> also takes raw JSON text. Input JCS cannot represent, such as a duplicate member name, is rejected with <code>input_not_canonicalizable</code> before any guest runs.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>That shipped in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/tag/v0.17.1">Traverse v0.17.1</a> (published 2026-11-16; the notes say Released 2026-11-13). Use <code>runtime-wasm-v0.17.1</code> and the 0.17.1 packages. v0.16.2 still has the stubs.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
